-- Re-pin the analysis and reply operation profiles for the rubric prompts.
--
-- Both developer prompts gained the constraints that were previously expressed
-- only in the validator:
--
-- 1. review-analysis-v1 now states the sentiment/valence bands that
--    AI_ANALYSIS_OUTPUT_SCHEMA's superRefine enforces (positive >= 20, neutral
--    -19..19, negative <= -20, mixed unconstrained). A model answering
--    'positive' with valence 15 was coherent, was what the prompt asked for,
--    and was rejected as output_invalid AFTER being fully billed.
-- 2. reply-suggestion-v1 now states the template selection rubric. The model
--    received four bare identifiers with no description of what any template
--    says and no precedence rule, and its selection was not merely unmeasured
--    but unmeasurable. The prose it selects between lives in
--    ai-reply-template-catalogue-v1.json; the rubric describes that prose.
--
-- The developer prompt is hashed, so editing it moves exactly four columns per
-- profile: prompt_digest, static_token_bearing_bytes (the prompt is embedded
-- verbatim in the canonical token-bearing material), static_token_bearing_digest
-- and profile_digest. Nothing else moves -- not the output schema, not the
-- artifact attestations, not the provider or routing digests.
--
-- static_token_bearing_bytes is load-bearing beyond identity: admit_ai_property_v1
-- recomputes maximumCostMicros in SQL from that column while the web computes it
-- from the compiled catalogue. If the row is not re-pinned the two disagree and
-- every operation is denied source_mismatch. Analysis 1081 -> 1253 bytes, reply
-- 906 -> 1373 bytes.
--
-- Fresh databases get these values from the unshipped 0046 seed, edited in place;
-- this migration is the forward fix for an already-migrated one and is a no-op
-- there. Same trigger-disable ceremony as 0065/0066/0067.--> statement-breakpoint
